  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Monday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: A Pacific cut off low will continue to\nmove inland and into SoCal this morning, and move into southern\nand central Nevada late this afternoon. Southwesterly flow ahead \nof the low will advect enough atmospheric moisture northward into\nCentral Nevada from the south-southwest to produce rain showers \nand thunderstorms beginning around midday today. The upper low \nwill continue to move northeastward thru the intermountain west \nwith showers and thunderstorms moving into eastern Nevada, \nincluding White Pine and Elko Counties, this evening and Tuesday \nmorning respectively. Activity looks to exit to the north-\nnortheast Tuesday night. The primary thunderstorm hazards will be\nwind gusts near 50 mph and dangerous cloud to ground lightning \nstrikes. Accumulating rainfall will be possible with the greatest\naccumulations expected in and near White Pine County.\nAccumulations between 0.10 and 0.25 inches are forecast with the\nhigher end accumulation situated over the high terrain and\nparticularly the Snake Range and Schell Creeks. \n\nFair weather returns Wednesday under weak upper level flow. By \nThursday afternoon, daytime high temperatures will be five to \nten degrees warmer than normal for this time of year. Breezes are\nalso expected to begin increasing Thursday in response to a\ndeepening Pacific trof offshore of the PacNW. Trof progresses east\nthru the PacNW Friday with the associated surface frontal boundary\nskirting northern Nevada. This will cause west-southwesterly winds\nto peak Friday afternoon in and near Humboldt County with gusts\naround 35 to 40 mph. Winds remain relatively breezy thru the \nweekend as the upper wave pattern remains progressive. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High confidence in shower and \nthunderstorm activity today thru Tuesday with moderate confidence\nin regard to rainfall accumulations. High confidence in the\nforecast from mid week thru the end of the week. Again opted for \na blend of various model guidance to produce a more accurate PoP \nand weather forecast than suggested by NBM thru the period.",
